 rose drug use went down and  there was no labor market impact so  we're hoping to see something similar in  Stockton you've got a wide span of  supporters everyone from Bernie Sanders  to Elon Musk to Richard Branson support  this initiative and it's beginning in  August right of this year ideally in  August we just had a program director  and so the next couple months she'll be  working with the community so the  community input figure out criteria and  selection work out the kinks and  hopefully by the time school starts  we're starting well what are some of the  criteria that that it will have if you  get the basic income what are some of  the things you put in the program to  avoid some of the criticisms  the ideas that it be as universal as  possible meaning you win me so someone  who's make $70,000 could get it someone  who makes seven thousand dollars would  be equally eligible to qualify I mean in  a second Tennant will be that it'll be  no strings attached that we trust that  people are smart in resilience and would  make the best decision for them their  families with the money understanding  that everyone's not going to do it right  I guess but that isn't happiness status  quo anyway it's a penalty if you don't  do it right you know it's 500 dollars no  strings attached the idea is to have a  conversation about kind of economic  system of this country but also  highlight stories are when suddenly a  smart 500 can do whether it's the  difference between the mother who's  above now pay for child care and get  back in the workforce or entrepreneur  who's able to work on their startup  ideas or artist it's really highlighting  the stories they've been a new face to  the conversation around poverty in the  country Stockton is not far from Silicon  Valley why hasn't it reaps some of the  benefits and that huge amount of wealth  that's in that area where we are working  on it I think part of it has been  oftentimes we've been so tunnel vision  and focus just just on stock because  we're close we're also a world away on  our diversity our average income the  educational attainment so we're working  on all those things just this month we  announced a 20 million dollar  scholarship program so now that every  kid in our school district could go to  school essentially tuition free yeah  really had quite the economic recovery  from 2012 right when you just graduated  from college let me ask you because the  president's really been touting a lot of  his initiatives over the past year have  you seen that come to fruition in your  city have you seen your citizens reap  the benefits of what the president said  he's been doing over the past year well  we're hoping to reap the benefits from  the opportunities on program but  honestly a lot of the time has been  spent kind of quelling fears especially  around immigration so for the past year  we've been having a conversation about  values because Stockland is one third  floor board meaning the majority of our  population are either immigrants or  children of immigrants so when we're  talking about taking people for families  and deporting people were doing raids it  causes a huge ripple effect in the  community so we've been filling those  effects and letting people know then  Stockton we're a city of immigrants  we're proud of our diversity and they're  and we're safe here what has been sorry  the reaction from the Attorney General  jeff Sessions to threatened to punish  Stockton for your immigration policy  which was interesting because it  mayor talked about jails and the city  doesn't operate jails the county does so  we sent the letter back nicely and said  you know we're a city of immigrants  we're not gonna stop the taint or frisk  someone just based on immigration status  and if you wouldn't talk about the jails  you got to talk about to the sheriff  which is a different office from the  City of Stockton and did they are you in  conversation with them or is it just a  one way thing from the Department of  Justice we respond when needed tell me  about those ripple effects you were  talking about in terms of the what the  president is saying to this immigrant  community and it causes a lot of fear so  then we see children are going to school  for example or we see folks are  reporting crimes for example or people  not engaging with government in a way  and for that to happen in Stockton it's  bad because again majority of our of our  population are either foreign-born or  their parents are meaning that they're  all that is our community and if they're  not part of it then we're not able to  have the civic life we need let me ask  about another thing you're trying which  is with paying what some people are  calling cash for criminals which is a  program that pays a stipend to young  people who were involved in crime yes a  program called advanced piece idea has  seven criteria the idea is that in most  urban cities a small percentage of  people commit the most violent crimes I  mean while the cops will still do their  things and gels will still exist but  while they're still in our community I  think we owe it to invest in them  I'm sorry --is that they're part of a  fellowship and if they meet certain  criterion after six months they're  eligible for a stipend based off work so  it goes to the ethos of having to earn  your keep but we're just making sure  we're doing what we can to invest in  everyone in our community especially  those who are the most marginalized and  oftentimes create the most harms is it  correct  I read that only seventeen point four  percent of Stockton residents have  bachelor's degrees that's absolutely  correct and we're working incredibly  hard to change that so I mentioned the  scholarship program we just launched to  make it so that young people see  colleges attainable in that's something  we're really focused on in the city  because that's going to be their  long-term key to our transformation you  are quite a success story yourself 26  year old mayor a Stanford grad what made  you decide that you were ready to be  mayor of a city like sucked I spent four  years on city council representing  everything I grew up in in those four  years working with the community we're  able to do things that sounds small but  we're huge we close down problem liquor  stores we open health clinics we got the  whole can  you organize around the South Stockton  promise on the reinvent stop stalking  idea and people would come to the  council meeting saying you only care  about self stop that you only care about  South Stockton at first I was like well  I'm doing my job but then I have was  listening and I said well maybe they're  saying the rest of the city could use  the same level of leadership so then I  decided to run for Mara 2016 1 and spend  a nice long fun and often hard journey  everything brought a lot of innovative  ideas and we'll be following them for  sure mara thank you so much for joining  us

